1. A ventilator system for maintaining stable pressure support ventilation of a patient, the ventilator system comprising:

a breathing gas delivery assembly configured to deliver pressurized breathing gas to an airway of the patient;

one or more sensors configured to generate measurement signals indicative of at least airway pressure, gas flow, and a breathing effort of the patient;

an actuator configured to regulate delivery of the pressurized breathing gas in response to one or more control signals; and

a controller operatively coupled to the one or more sensors and the actuator, the controller comprising one or more processors configured to:

receive the measurement signals and the one or more control signals during pressure support ventilation;

determine a patient breathing effort signal from the measurement signals;

fit a controlled mechanical ventilation model to the patient breathing effort signal and to the one or more control signals, the controlled mechanical ventilation model representing a dynamic relationship among ventilator control, delivered pressure or flow, and respiratory system response;

estimate, from the fitted controlled mechanical ventilation model, one or more parameters indicative of ventilatory stability;

determine, based at least in part on the one or more parameters, whether the pressure support ventilation is approaching or exhibiting instability; and

adjust at least one pressure support ventilation setting to maintain stable pressure support ventilation.

2. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the patient breathing effort signal comprises an estimated inspiratory muscle pressure, diaphragm electrical activity, esophageal pressure, airway occlusion pressure, or a surrogate effort signal derived from flow and pressure measurements.

3. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein fitting the controlled mechanical ventilation model comprises identifying model parameters using measured ventilator output signals and corresponding patient effort signals over a plurality of respiratory cycles.

4. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the one or more parameters indicative of ventilatory stability comprise a gain, delay, phase margin, damping ratio, loop response parameter, patient-ventilator interaction parameter, or respiratory drive response parameter.

5. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to distinguish patient-driven variation from ventilator-induced variation by comparing the measured breathing effort with the response predicted by the fitted controlled mechanical ventilation model.

6. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein adjusting the at least one pressure support ventilation setting comprises modifying pressure support level, rise time, cycling threshold, trigger sensitivity, expiratory positive airway pressure, backup rate, inspiratory time limit, or a combination thereof.

7. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to update the fitted controlled mechanical ventilation model repeatedly during ventilation to account for changes in patient respiratory mechanics or respiratory drive.

8. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to reduce pressure support when the fitted model indicates excessive ventilatory gain or an elevated likelihood of periodic breathing, overshoot, undershoot, central apnea, or patient-ventilator asynchrony.

9. The ventilator system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to increase or maintain pressure support when the fitted model indicates stable assisted ventilation and inadequate patient ventilation or excessive patient effort.

10. A method of operating a ventilator to maintain stable pressure support ventilation, the method comprising:

delivering pressure support ventilation to a patient;

measuring airway pressure, gas flow, ventilator control signals, and a signal representative of patient breathing effort;

fitting a controlled mechanical ventilation model to the measured patient breathing effort and the ventilator control signals;

estimating ventilatory stability from the fitted controlled mechanical ventilation model; and

automatically modifying one or more pressure support ventilation parameters based on the estimated ventilatory stability to reduce unstable ventilation while maintaining ventilatory assistance.

11. The method of claim 10, wherein the controlled mechanical ventilation model is fitted using recursive estimation, adaptive filtering, system identification, state-space estimation, least-squares estimation, Kalman filtering, or machine-learning-assisted parameter estimation.

12. The method of claim 10, further comprising generating an alarm, notification, or clinician-readable stability index when the fitted controlled mechanical ventilation model indicates an unstable or marginally stable ventilatory condition.

13. The method of claim 10, wherein automatically modifying the one or more pressure support ventilation parameters is constrained by predetermined safety limits including maximum pressure, minimum ventilation, apnea backup criteria, or clinician-set boundaries.

14. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a ventilator, cause the ventilator to:

receive measured respiratory signals and ventilator control signals during pressure support ventilation;

derive a breathing effort signal from the measured respiratory signals;

fit a controlled mechanical ventilation model to the breathing effort signal and the ventilator control signals;

determine a stability metric from the fitted model; and

control at least one ventilator setting as a function of the stability metric to maintain stable pressure support ventilation.
